- Queue Interface in java is present in java.util package.
- It extends the Collection interface
  and provides methods for inserting, removing, and inspecting elements in a queue.
- If Follows FIFO - First-In-First-Out Approach.
- Supports insertion at the rear and deletion from the front.


- offer(E e): Adds an element to the queue. It returns true if the element was successfully added,
              or false if the queue is full

- poll(): Retrieves and removes the front element of the queue. If the queue is empty, it returns null

- peek(): Retrieves but does not remove the front element of the queue.
          If the queue is empty, it returns null.

- remove(): Removes and returns the front element.
            Throws an exception (NoSuchElementException) if the queue is empty.

- isEmpty(): Checks if the queue is empty.

- size(): Returns the number of elements in the queue.

- element(): Retrieves but does not remove the front element of the queue. Throws an exception (NoSuchElementException) if the queue is empty.

[ peek() and element() are same but usecase of Exception Handling is Different ]

[ poll() and remove() are same but Uscase of Exception Handling is different ]



